\title{frmtmb.spline: Spline Curves and Curve Inference for 'frmtmb' Models}
\description{
Two things a fitted curve needs after it is fitted. First, curve inference: a smooth evaluated on a grid with pointwise and SIMULTANEOUS confidence bands, its first and second derivatives with delta-method standard errors, and the features movement papers report, the time of a peak and the time a curve crosses a level, each with a standard error from the implicit-function delta method. The simultaneous band is the max-deviation simulation of Ruppert, Wand and Carroll (2003), the same construction 'gratia' uses on 'mgcv' fits, and it is checked against 'gratia' inside its Monte Carlo error. Second, a Royston and Parmar (2002) flexible parametric survival family, which writes the log cumulative hazard as a natural cubic spline in log time. It is parameterized exactly as 'flexsurv::flexsurvspline' parameterizes it, so the two log likelihoods are the same number rather than two numbers that ought to agree, and covariates reach the spline coefficients as proportional hazards on the first and as time-varying effects on the rest.
}
\section{What this package reads that frmtmb does not promise}{

Two of the three things this package reads off a fitted object are
documented seams and one is not. It is named here so that a reader
does not have to discover it from the sources.
\itemize{
\item \code{fit$estimates} and \code{fit$obj} both have precedent: the
\code{frmtmb::frmtmb-extension-api} example reads \code{fit$estimates}, and
\code{frmtmb.sample::frm_sample()} reads \code{fit$obj}.
\item \strong{\code{fit$cache$Vjoint} has no precedent and is an internal.} It is
the memo written by frmtmb's \code{get_joint_cov()}, which is not
exported and not documented, and neither the slot nor its
\code{list(V =, names =)} shape appears anywhere in frmtmb's own
documentation. \code{frm_curve()} reads it because the alternative,
recomputing the joint covariance here, was both far slower and
WRONG on an autoscaled fit.
}

The consequences of that reach are bounded and are set out in full
under \code{\link[=frm_curve]{frm_curve()}}, section "The one internal this reaches into": a
change to the name or the shape costs speed, a change to the meaning
is caught by the covariance check that every call makes, and absence
is the ordinary case on a fresh fit and is handled by warming the
cache first. None of the three can produce a wrong number.

\code{dev/spline-seam-proposal.md} Part 1a asks core for an exported
accessor, which would retire the reach entirely.
}

\section{Two limits that are core's to fix}{

\itemize{
\item \code{logLik()} and \code{AIC()} on a \code{\link[=royston_parmar]{royston_parmar()}} fit report whatever
the optimizer reached, and neither can be gated from an extension:
\code{logLik()} reads \code{object$opt$objective} and the family protocol has
no hook that runs when a fit finishes. Where the likelihood was
floored, those two numbers are wrong and say nothing.
\code{\link[=rp_floored]{rp_floored()}} refuses, and \code{\link[=frm_curve]{frm_curve()}} calls it, but a user who
reads \code{AIC()} and nothing else gets no signal. The fix is core's:
an \code{lccdf} slot, or a post-fit family hook.
\item A mapped random-effect block is untested here, because
\code{frmtmb::frmtmb_control()} takes no \code{map} argument and there is
therefore no supported route to one. The nearest reachable
analogue is a distributional parameter held fixed, which sets
\code{betad_fixed_idx} and takes the same index-remapping path:
\code{bf(y ~ s(x, k = 8), sigma = 0.5)} works at a measured
\code{cov_rel_error} of 1.55e-15.
}
